          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is high blood pressure, and how is it different from one high reading?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure, also called hypertension, means pressure in the arteries stays raised over time. Blood pressure is written as two numbers: the top (systolic) number is the pressure when the heart beats, and the bottom (diastolic) number is the pressure between beats. Both are measured in millimeters of mercury. (Source: \"Hypertension (high blood pressure)\", General clinical reference.)\n\nOne high reading is not enough to call it hypertension. Pressure changes all day with activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep, and time of day. It is usually lowest during sleep and rises before waking. Because of this, a diagnosis depends on repeated measurements, and a single high reading in a clinic is not a diagnosis by itself. (Same source.)\n\nThe same source notes that high blood pressure usually causes no symptoms. It is most often found during routine checks, which is why screening exists.\n\nThe passages I retrieved do not cover what the readings mean for a specific person, what numbers count as high, or how it is treated. If you have a reading you are worried about, or you have had high readings more than once, a clinician can repeat the measurements and explain them for your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
